diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the reported outage between PC0 (20.1.1.10) and PC12 (120.1.1.10). End-to-end reachability is currently healthy: both directions ping successfully with 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTTs (pc0→pc12 avg ~0.94 ms; pc12→pc0 avg ~0.75 ms).

The core control plane is also healthy. On r1, OSPF adjacencies are Full to its backbone neighbors: 39.1.1.1 on eth2 (31.1.1.0/8) and 112.1.1.1 on eth1 (111.1.1.0/24). On r11, the OSPF neighbor to r1 on eth1 is Full. This indicates no OSPF area/backbone break on the PC0→core→right‑side path at present.

All key data-plane links along the path show carrier up: r1 eth4 (to PC0) and eth1 (to r11) report “Link detected: yes”; r11 eth1 (to r1) and eth0 (to r12) are up; r12 eth0 (to r11) and eth1 (to PC12) are up. PC0 and PC12 host NICs are up with correct default gateways.

Localization: No failed interface or adjacency observed on r1–r11 (B/111.1.1.x), r11–r12 (EE/112.1.1.x), r1–PC0 (E/20.0.0.0/8), or r12–PC12 (GG/120.0.0.0/8).

Root cause: No current link-down fault; if there was a maintenance-related transient, it has recovered.

Corrective fix: None required now. Recommend monitoring OSPF logs/telemetry and collecting exact outage timestamps if the issue recurs, then correlating with interface/OSPF events on r1, r11, and r12 to identify any intermittent flaps.
